    <!-- Why Data Splitting Matters -->
    <section class="section" id="why-splitting">
      <span class="section-label">Foundation Concepts</span>
      <h2>Why Data Splitting Matters</h2>
      <p>The most common mistake in machine learning is building a model on training data and then testing it on the same data. This creates an illusion of performance that doesn't translate to real-world situations. Data splitting is fundamental to creating models that actually generalize to new, unseen data.</p>

      <p>When you train a model on all available data, it can memorize patterns and even noise specific to that dataset. This phenomenon, called overfitting, produces excellent training accuracy but poor real-world performance. By separating data into distinct sets, we create a more realistic evaluation environment that mirrors how the model will be used in production.</p>

