    <!-- Three-Way Split -->
    <section class="section" id="three-way">
      <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
      <h2>The Three-Way Split: Train, Validation, and Test</h2>
      <p>Modern machine learning practice uses three distinct datasets, each with a specific role in model development and evaluation. Understanding these roles is essential for building production-ready models.</p>

      <p><strong>Training Set (60%):</strong> This is where your model learns. The optimization algorithm adjusts model weights to minimize loss on this data. It's the "textbook" your model studies from.</p>

      <p><strong>Validation Set (15-20%):</strong> Used during training to monitor performance on unseen data. You use validation metrics to make decisions: Should we stop training? Should we adjust the learning rate? Which model architecture performs best? The model never learns weights directly from this set, but your decisions as an engineer do.</p>

      <p><strong>Test Set (15-20%):</strong> The final, untouched evaluation set. You only look at test performance once, at the very end. This gives you an unbiased estimate of how well your model will perform on completely new data in production.</p>

      <h3>Implementation with scikit-learn</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">model_selection</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Load your data</span>
<span class="va">X</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([...])  <span class="cm"># Features</span>
<span class="va">y</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([...])  <span class="cm"># Target</span>

<span class="cm"># First split: 80% train+val, 20% test</span>
<span class="va">X_temp</span>, <span class="va">X_test</span>, <span class="va">y_temp</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>(
    <span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">y</span>,
    <span class="dt">test_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.2</span>,
    <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Second split: divide remaining into train (75%) and validation (25%)</span>
<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">X_val</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="va">y_val</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>(
    <span class="va">X_temp</span>, <span class="va">y_temp</span>,
    <span class="dt">test_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.25</span>,  <span class="cm"># 25% of 80% = 20%</span>
    <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Results: Train 60%, Val 20%, Test 20%</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Train: {len(X_train)/len(X):.1%}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Val: {len(X_val)/len(X):.1%}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Test: {len(X_test)/len(X):.1%}"</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- Stratified Sampling -->
    <section class="section" id="stratification">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span>
      <h2>Stratified Sampling for Imbalanced Data</h2>
      <p>When your target variable has imbalanced classes (e.g., 95% negative, 5% positive), random splitting can produce train/test sets with different class distributions. This creates a misleading evaluation. Stratified sampling ensures each split has approximately the same class distribution as the original dataset.</p>

      <p>For example, if your original dataset has 10% positive cases, stratified sampling ensures your train, validation, and test sets each have approximately 10% positive cases. This is especially critical in classification problems with rare events like fraud detection or disease diagnosis.</p>

      <h3>Stratified Split Implementation</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">model_selection</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>

<span class="cm"># Imbalanced classification dataset</span>
<span class="va">y</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>])  <span class="cm"># 10% positive</span>

<span class="cm"># WITHOUT stratification (risky!)</span>
<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">X_test</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>(
    <span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">y</span>, <span class="dt">test_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.3</span>, <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>
)
<span class="cm"># Test set might have 0%, 20%, or 33% positive cases - unreliable!</span>

<span class="cm"># WITH stratification (recommended)</span>
<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">X_test</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>(
    <span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">y</span>,
    <span class="dt">test_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.3</span>,
    <span class="dt">stratify</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">y</span>,  <span class="cm"># KEY: stratify by target variable</span>
    <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>
)
<span class="cm"># Both train and test will have ~10% positive cases</span></code></pre>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Always use stratified sampling for classification problems, especially with imbalanced classes. For regression, consider stratifying on binned target values if there's significant skew in the target distribution.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Cross-Validation -->
    <section class="section" id="cross-validation">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span>
      <h2>K-Fold Cross-Validation</h2>
      <p>When datasets are small, a single train/test split may not provide a reliable performance estimate. Cross-validation addresses this by using multiple train/test splits and averaging results. The most common approach is k-fold cross-validation, where k is typically 5 or 10.</p>

      <p>In k-fold CV, you divide data into k equal partitions (folds). You then train k models: each time, one fold serves as the test set while the remaining k-1 folds serve as training data. The final performance metric is the average across all k runs. This approach maximizes use of your data while providing more robust performance estimates with confidence intervals.</p>

      <h3>K-Fold Cross-Validation Example</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">model_selection</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">cross_val_score</span>, <span class="fn">KFold</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">ensemble</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">RandomForestClassifier</span>

<span class="cm"># Create 5-fold cross-validation object</span>
<span class="va">kfold</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">KFold</span>(<span class="dt">n_splits</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="dt">shuffle</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">True</span>, <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>)

<span class="cm"># Initialize model</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">RandomForestClassifier</span>(<span class="dt">n_estimators</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>)

<span class="cm"># Perform cross-validation - returns array of 5 scores</span>
<span class="va">scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">cross_val_score</span>(
    <span class="va">model</span>, <span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">y</span>,
    <span class="dt">cv</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">kfold</span>,
    <span class="dt">scoring</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'accuracy'</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Analyze results</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Fold scores: {scores}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Mean accuracy: {scores.mean():.3f}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Std deviation: {scores.std():.3f}"</span>)
<span class="cm"># Output: Mean: 0.872 +/- 0.034</span></code></pre>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <strong>When to use K-Fold CV:</strong> This is ideal for small to medium datasets (< 100k samples) where you want robust performance estimates. For very large datasets, a single train/validation/test split is more efficient.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Real-World Application -->
    <section class="section" id="application">
      <span class="section-label">Real-World Application</span>
      <h2>Complete Workflow Example: Customer Churn Prediction</h2>
      <p>Let's apply these concepts to a real-world problem: predicting customer churn at a telecom company. You have 10,000 customer records with 50 features and want to predict which customers will leave.</p>

      <h3>Production-Ready Split Strategy</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">model_selection</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>, <span class="fn">StratifiedKFold</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">preprocessing</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">StandardScaler</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">ensemble</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">GradientBoostingClassifier</span>

<span class="cm"># Step 1: Create hold-out test set (20%, untouched until final evaluation)</span>
<span class="va">X_temp</span>, <span class="va">X_test</span>, <span class="va">y_temp</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>(
    <span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">y</span>,
    <span class="dt">test_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.2</span>,
    <span class="dt">stratify</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">y</span>,
    <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Step 2: Split remaining 80% into train (75%) and validation (25%)</span>
<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">X_val</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="va">y_val</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>(
    <span class="va">X_temp</span>, <span class="va">y_temp</span>,
    <span class="dt">test_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.25</span>,
    <span class="dt">stratify</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">y_temp</span>,
    <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Step 3: Preprocessing - IMPORTANT: fit ONLY on training data</span>
<span class="va">scaler</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">StandardScaler</span>()
<span class="va">X_train_scaled</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">scaler</span>.<span class="fn">fit_transform</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>)
<span class="va">X_val_scaled</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">scaler</span>.<span class="fn">transform</span>(<span class="va">X_val</span>)  <span class="cm"># transform only!</span>
<span class="va">X_test_scaled</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">scaler</span>.<span class="fn">transform</span>(<span class="va">X_test</span>)

<span class="cm"># Step 4: Train model</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">GradientBoostingClassifier</span>(<span class="dt">n_estimators</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>)
<span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train_scaled</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)

<span class="cm"># Step 5: Use validation set to evaluate</span>
<span class="va">val_accuracy</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">score</span>(<span class="va">X_val_scaled</span>, <span class="va">y_val</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Validation accuracy: {val_accuracy:.3f}"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Step 6: FINAL evaluation on test set (do this only once!)</span>
<span class="va">test_accuracy</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">score</span>(<span class="va">X_test_scaled</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Test accuracy: {test_accuracy:.3f}"</span>)</code></pre>

      <p>This workflow demonstrates several critical principles: stratified sampling ensures representative splits, preprocessing is fit only on training data, and the test set is evaluated only once at the very end.</p>
    </section>

